   "text": {
    "L1": "Capacity tells us how much a container can hold. We can measure it in litres or millilitres.",
    "L2": "One litre equals 1,000 millilitres. So we can replace every litre with 1,000 millilitres.",
    "L3": "For mixed units, change the litres to millilitres and add the remaining millilitres.",
    "L4": "That makes 1 litre 250 millilitres equal to 1,250 millilitres, not 1,250 litres. The unit must match the answer.",
    "L5": "Zeros hold empty places. For example, 2 litres 5 millilitres becomes 2,005 millilitres.",
    "L6": "Now use the worked example: 1 litre 500 millilitres becomes 1,500 millilitres.",
    "L7": "Four 250 millilitre glasses make 1,000 millilitres. Two more glasses make the other 500 millilitres.",
    "L8": "So 1 litre 500 millilitres fills 6 glasses of 250 millilitres.",
    "L9": "To compare capacities, write them in the same unit. Now choose the correct conversion."
   },
